    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "international",
        "title": "Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations Article 31 — Immunity from Jurisdiction",
        "summary": "Article 31(1) of the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations (1961) grants diplomatic agents immunity from criminal, civil, and administrative jurisdiction of the receiving state. This immunity has been invoked by diplomats accused of trafficking domestic workers, shielding them from prosecution. No exception exists for trafficking or forced labor. The only remedies are waiver by the sending state (Article 32) or declaring the diplomat persona non grata (Article 9). Between 2000 and 2020, the US State Department documented over 150 complaints of domestic worker abuse by diplomats, with zero criminal prosecutions due to immunity claims.",
        "law": "Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations",
        "year": 1961,
        "source": "UN Treaty Series / US State Department / Human Rights Watch",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "US",
        "title": "Foreign Sovereign Immunities Act (FSIA) — State Immunity in Trafficking Lawsuits",
        "summary": "The Foreign Sovereign Immunities Act (28 U.S.C. §§ 1602-1611) grants foreign states immunity from US court jurisdiction. Trafficking victims have attempted to sue foreign governments for complicity in trafficking but face FSIA barriers. The 'commercial activity' exception (§1605(a)(2)) has been used in some cases involving state-owned enterprises. The 2016 JASTA amendment created a terrorism exception but no trafficking exception exists. Victims of government-facilitated trafficking through labor export programs generally cannot sue origin or destination governments in US courts.",
        "law": "Foreign Sovereign Immunities Act, 28 U.S.C. §§ 1602-1611",
        "year": 1976,
        "source": "US Congress / Restatement (Fourth) of Foreign Relations Law",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "US",
        "title": "TVPRA Section 1595A — Government Contractor Liability for Trafficking",
        "summary": "Section 1595A of the Trafficking Victims Protection Reauthorization Act (codified at 18 U.S.C. § 1595A, added by the 2008 TVPRA) permits civil actions against government contractors and subcontractors who engage in trafficking. Created in response to documented trafficking by US military contractors in Iraq and Afghanistan (KBR/Halliburton subcontractors using Nepali and Filipino workers in forced labor conditions). Provides for compensatory damages, punitive damages, and attorney fees. Has been used in cases involving defense contractors, USAID grantees, and diplomatic construction projects.",
        "law": "18 U.S.C. § 1595A (TVPRA 2008)",
        "year": 2008,
        "source": "US Congress / DOJ / DOD Inspector General",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "UK",
        "title": "Modern Slavery Act 2015 Section 54 — Government Procurement Obligations",
        "summary": "Section 54 of the UK Modern Slavery Act 2015 requires commercial organizations with annual turnover exceeding GBP 36 million to publish annual transparency statements on steps taken to ensure slavery and trafficking are not occurring in their supply chains. The UK Government committed in 2019 to publish its own Section 54 statement covering central government procurement. However, the statement was not published until 2020 and was criticized by the Anti-Slavery Commissioner as 'lacking meaningful detail.' Local government procurement is not covered, creating a gap in public sector supply chain oversight.",
        "law": "Modern Slavery Act 2015, Section 54",
        "year": 2015,
        "source": "UK Parliament / Independent Anti-Slavery Commissioner / TISC Report",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "EU",
        "title": "EU Anti-Trafficking Directive 2011/36/EU Article 8 — Non-Prosecution of Victims",
        "summary": "Article 8 of EU Directive 2011/36/EU provides that EU member states shall take measures to ensure that national authorities do not prosecute or apply penalties to trafficking victims for their involvement in criminal activities they were compelled to commit. Implementation has been inconsistent: GRETA evaluations found that as of 2023, 12 EU member states had not fully transposed Article 8 into national law. Victims continued to be prosecuted for immigration offenses, petty theft, and prostitution across the EU, undermining their willingness to cooperate with trafficking investigations.",
        "law": "EU Directive 2011/36/EU, Article 8",
        "year": 2011,
        "source": "European Parliament / GRETA / EU Anti-Trafficking Coordinator",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "JO",
        "title": "Jordan Labour Law Exclusion of Domestic Workers — Article 3(b)",
        "summary": "Article 3(b) of Jordan's Labour Code (Law No. 8 of 1996, as amended) historically excluded domestic workers, agricultural workers, and family enterprise workers from labor law protections including minimum wage, maximum working hours, and occupational safety standards. A 2008 regulation provided limited protections for domestic workers but did not extend the full Labour Code. This exclusion effectively left 50,000+ migrant domestic workers (primarily from the Philippines, Sri Lanka, and Indonesia) without recourse to labor courts for abuse, wage theft, or trafficking. Jordan adopted a special regulation for domestic workers in 2009, but enforcement remained minimal.",
        "law": "Jordan Labour Code, Law No. 8 of 1996, Article 3(b)",
        "year": 1996,
        "source": "ILO / Tamkeen / Mizan Law Group",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "BD",
        "title": "Bangladesh EPZ Labour Law — Trade Union Prohibition Until 2019",
        "summary": "The Bangladesh Export Processing Zones Authority Act 1980 (amended 2004) prohibited trade union formation in export processing zones, replacing unions with 'Worker Welfare Associations' controlled by BEPZA. Workers in 8 EPZs (employing 500,000+, majority women) could not collectively bargain, strike, or affiliate with national trade federations. ILO repeatedly found this violated C87 (Freedom of Association) and C98 (Right to Organise). The 2019 Bangladesh EPZ Labour Act finally permitted limited union activity but required 30% worker support threshold and BEPZA approval for formation.",
        "law": "Bangladesh EPZ Authority Act 1980; EPZ Labour Act 2019",
        "year": 1980,
        "source": "ILO CEACR / ITUC / Solidarity Center",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "VN",
        "title": "Vietnam Labour Code 2019 — Free Trade Zone Labour Exemptions",
        "summary": "Vietnam's revised Labour Code (effective January 2021) maintained special provisions for export processing and industrial zones that weakened overtime protections. Workers in EPZs could be required to work up to 300 hours of overtime per year (vs. 200 hours standard) with 'zone authority' approval. The distinction created a two-tier labor rights system exploited by garment and electronics manufacturers. Vietnamese Labour Code Article 107 allowed additional overtime during 'extraordinary circumstances' — a provision EPZ employers invoked routinely.",
        "law": "Vietnam Labour Code 2019, Articles 107-109",
        "year": 2019,
        "source": "ILO Hanoi / MOLISA / Vietnam General Confederation of Labour",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "PH",
        "title": "Philippines Free Trade Zone Exemptions — PEZA Act of 1995",
        "summary": "The Philippine Economic Zone Authority (PEZA) Act (RA 7916, 1995) granted companies in Special Economic Zones (ecozones) fiscal incentives including income tax holidays, duty-free imports, and expedited business permits. While labor law technically applied in ecozones, PEZA's mandate to attract foreign investment created institutional reluctance to enforce labor standards. DOLE inspections in ecozones required PEZA coordination, adding a gatekeeper layer. A 2016 DOLE audit found 42% of ecozone companies violated overtime provisions and 28% had wage order non-compliance.",
        "law": "RA 7916 (PEZA Act), 1995",
        "year": 1995,
        "source": "PEZA / DOLE / KMU / Ecumenical Institute for Labor Education and Research",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "US",
        "title": "13th Amendment Prison Labor Exception — Trafficking Implications",
        "summary": "The 13th Amendment to the US Constitution (1865) abolishes slavery and involuntary servitude 'except as a punishment for crime whereof the party shall have been duly convicted.' This exception permits prison labor at little or no pay. In 2022, 800,000+ incarcerated individuals worked in prison labor programs earning $0.13-$0.52/hour on average. Private companies including Whole Foods, McDonald's, Victoria's Secret, and BP have used prison labor. Immigration detainees — who have not been convicted of crimes — have been required to perform facility maintenance at $1/day under detention 'voluntary work programs,' creating a trafficking nexus. Campaigns to amend the 13th Amendment failed in Colorado (2018 ballot passed) and nationwide (2020 legislation stalled).",
        "law": "US Constitution, 13th Amendment (1865)",
        "year": 1865,
        "source": "ACLU / Worth Rises / US Bureau of Justice Statistics",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "US",
        "title": "Executive Order 13627 — Strengthening Protections Against Trafficking in Federal Contracting",
        "summary": "EO 13627 (2012) and implementing FAR rules (FAR 52.222-50) prohibit federal contractors and subcontractors from engaging in trafficking-related practices including charging recruitment fees, destroying identity documents, and restricting workers' access to return transportation. Applies to contracts exceeding $550,000 performed outside the US. Contractors must implement compliance plans and certify compliance. In 2019, DOD OIG found 60% of audited defense contractors in the Middle East lacked required anti-trafficking compliance plans. Maximum penalty is contract termination and debarment.",
        "law": "Executive Order 13627; FAR 52.222-50",
        "year": 2012,
        "source": "White House / FAR Council / DOD OIG / State Department",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "international",
        "title": "ILO Convention 181 — Private Employment Agencies Convention (1997)",
        "summary": "ILO C181 establishes that private employment agencies shall not charge workers directly or indirectly for recruitment services (Article 7). Only 35 countries had ratified C181 as of 2023. Major labor-sending countries (Philippines, Bangladesh, Nepal, Pakistan, Indonesia, India) have NOT ratified, allowing legal recruitment fees. Major receiving countries (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Qatar, Kuwait) have also not ratified. This ratification gap means the 'employer pays' principle has no binding legal force in the corridors where trafficking through recruitment fraud is most prevalent.",
        "law": "ILO Convention 181",
        "year": 1997,
        "source": "ILO NORMLEX / Fair Recruitment Initiative",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "US",
        "title": "State Anti-Trafficking Laws — Variable Enforcement Creating Safe Havens",
        "summary": "All 50 US states have enacted anti-trafficking statutes, but enforcement varies dramatically. A 2019 Polaris Project analysis found that 13 states had zero labor trafficking convictions between 2007 and 2019. Texas, California, and New York accounted for 62% of all state-level trafficking prosecutions. States without dedicated anti-trafficking task forces averaged 0.3 convictions per year vs. 4.7 per year in states with task forces. Resource allocation ranged from $0 (Wyoming, Montana) to $57 million (California) in dedicated anti-trafficking funding.",
        "law": "State anti-trafficking statutes (various)",
        "year": 2007,
        "source": "Polaris Project / NCSL / DOJ BJA Anti-Trafficking Task Forces",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "QA",
        "title": "Qatar Labour Law Article 65 — Employer Consent for Exit Permits (Pre-Reform)",
        "summary": "Prior to 2020 reforms, Qatar Labour Law Article 65 required workers to obtain employer-issued exit permits (No Objection Certificates, NOCs) to leave the country. Employers used this provision to prevent workers from absconding, fleeing abuse, or returning home at will — constituting restriction of movement under ILO forced labor indicators. An estimated 174,000 exit permit requests were denied or ignored in 2018. Qatar announced NOC abolition in January 2020 (Ministerial Decision No. 95 of 2019), but implementation was staggered and domestic workers were initially excluded.",
        "law": "Qatar Labour Law No. 14 of 2004, Article 65; Ministerial Decision No. 95/2019",
        "year": 2004,
        "source": "ILO Qatar / ITUC / Human Rights Watch",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "US",
        "title": "Bilateral Labor Agreements — Missing Enforcement Provisions (US-Mexico, US-Guatemala)",
        "summary": "US bilateral labor cooperation agreements with Mexico (2015 MOU) and Guatemala (2019 Asylum Cooperative Agreement) contained no binding worker protection enforcement provisions. The US-Mexico seasonal worker MOU addressed H-2A/H-2B programs but had no joint inspection mechanism, no shared complaints database, and no reciprocal enforcement authority. Workers recruited in Mexico for US agricultural work had no bilateral mechanism to pursue wage theft claims once they returned to Mexico. The 2019 Guatemala agreement was suspended in 2021 after criticism that it traded asylum access for labor market access without worker protections.",
        "law": "US-Mexico Labor MOU (2015); US-Guatemala ACA (2019)",
        "year": 2015,
        "source": "US State Department / DOL / Centro de los Derechos del Migrante",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "AU",
        "title": "Australia Fair Work Act — Exclusion of Visa-Contingent Workers from Full Protections",
        "summary": "While Australia's Fair Work Act 2009 technically applies to all workers regardless of visa status, visa-contingent workers (457, TSS, student, WHM visas) face structural barriers to enforcement. Workers who report underpayment risk visa cancellation if found to have breached visa conditions (e.g., exceeding hour limits on student visas). The Migrant Workers' Taskforce (2019) recommended statutory protections against visa cancellation for workers who report exploitation, but the recommendation was not legislated until the Migration Amendment (Strengthening Employer Compliance) Act 2024.",
        "law": "Fair Work Act 2009; Migration Act 1958",
        "year": 2009,
        "source": "Migrant Workers' Taskforce Report (2019) / FWO / Senate Committee",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "TH",
        "title": "Thailand Royal Ordinance on Managing Foreign Workers B.E. 2560 — Registration Barriers",
        "summary": "Thailand's Royal Ordinance on Managing the Work of Foreign Nationals B.E. 2560 (2017) imposed severe penalties on undocumented workers (imprisonment up to 5 years, fines up to 100,000 THB/$2,800) but created registration processes requiring employer cooperation. Workers could not self-register; employers controlled the MOU registration process. This created a structural incentive for employers to keep workers undocumented as a control mechanism. The Migrant Working Group documented that 40% of Myanmar workers in Thailand were unable to register because their employers refused to participate in the process.",
        "law": "Royal Ordinance on Managing Foreign Workers B.E. 2560 (2017)",
        "year": 2017,
        "source": "Migrant Working Group / ILO Bangkok / IOM Thailand",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "international",
        "title": "Palermo Protocol Article 6 — State Obligation to Protect Trafficking Victims",
        "summary": "Article 6 of the Protocol to Prevent, Suppress and Punish Trafficking in Persons (Palermo Protocol, 2000) establishes state obligations to protect trafficking victims including privacy, information, recovery assistance, and the possibility of compensation. However, Article 6 uses permissive language ('shall consider implementing') rather than mandatory obligations for victim protection measures. UNODC found that as of 2022, 42% of States Parties had not implemented Article 6 provisions on victim compensation, and 35% lacked formal victim identification procedures.",
        "law": "Protocol to Prevent, Suppress and Punish Trafficking in Persons, Article 6",
        "year": 2000,
        "source": "UNODC / UN General Assembly A/RES/55/25",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "EU",
        "title": "EU Employers' Sanctions Directive 2009/52/EC — Employer Penalties for Hiring Undocumented Workers",
        "summary": "EU Directive 2009/52/EC establishes minimum penalties for employers who hire irregularly staying third-country nationals. Article 9 provides aggravated penalties when employment involves 'particularly exploitative working conditions.' However, the directive focuses on immigration enforcement rather than worker protection. PICUM (Platform for International Cooperation on Undocumented Migrants) noted that the directive's implementation across EU states prioritized employer fines over worker rights, creating a 'deportation pipeline' where victims of exploitation were removed before accessing justice. Only 8 EU states created explicit 'firewalls' between labor inspection and immigration enforcement.",
        "law": "EU Directive 2009/52/EC",
        "year": 2009,
        "source": "European Parliament / PICUM / FRA",
    },
    {
        "type": "law",
        "jurisdiction": "US",
        "title": "TVPA § 107(e) — T-Visa and Continued Presence (Structural Limitations)",
        "summary": "The TVPA provides T-visas for trafficking victims (capped at 5,000/year) and Continued Presence during investigations. However, structural barriers limit access: T-visa adjudication takes 18-24 months average; only 2,300 T-visas were approved in FY 2019 (vs. 5,000 cap); victims must demonstrate 'reasonable cooperation' with law enforcement (creating dependency on investigators); and victims of labor trafficking receive T-visas at far lower rates than sex trafficking victims (35% vs. 65% of approvals). The cooperation requirement has been criticized for re-traumatizing victims and excluding those whose traffickers were never prosecuted.",
        "law": "Trafficking Victims Protection Act, § 107(e); 8 U.S.C. § 1101(a)(15)(T)",
        "year": 2000,
        "source": "USCIS / Polaris Project / National Immigrant Justice Center",
    },

